### What is the Linked List Cycle problem?

The problem gives the head of a singly linked list and asks whether any node's next pointer points back to an earlier node, creating a cycle. The solution must use constant extra space. This is the classic application of Floyd's tortoise and hare algorithm.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LinkedListCycle {
    static class ListNode {
        int val;
        ListNode next;
        ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
    }

    public boolean hasCycle(ListNode head) {
        ListNode slow = head;
        ListNode fast = head;

        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;          // one step
            fast = fast.next.next;     // two steps
            if (slow == fast) return true; // met inside cycle
        }
        return false; // fast reached the end
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Build: T1 -> T2 -> T3 -> T4 -> T2 (cycle)
        ListNode t1 = new ListNode(1);
        ListNode t2 = new ListNode(2);
        ListNode t3 = new ListNode(3);
        ListNode t4 = new ListNode(4);
        t1.next = t2; t2.next = t3; t3.next = t4; t4.next = t2;

        System.out.println("Has cycle: " + new LinkedListCycle().hasCycle(t1));
    }
}
```

```text
Has cycle: true
```

### Why does Floyd's algorithm work?

The key is the relative speed argument. Once both pointers are inside the cycle, the fast pointer gains exactly one node on the slow pointer each step. If the cycle has length C, the gap between them decreases by 1 each step, so within C steps the gap becomes zero and they land on the same node. The fast pointer cannot skip over the slow pointer because the gap changes by exactly 1.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RelativeSpeedProof {
    // Suppose slow is at position s, fast at position f in the cycle
    // gap = (f - s) mod C
    // Each step: slow moves 1, fast moves 2
    // New gap = (f+2 - s-1) mod C = gap + 1 mod C
    // But relative to slow, fast advances 1 per step
    // So gap decreases by 1 each step (from slow's perspective)
    // After C steps, gap = 0 => they meet

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Fast gains 1 node per step on slow.");
        System.out.println("In a cycle of length C, they meet within C steps.");
        System.out.println("Fast cannot skip slow: gap changes by exactly 1.");
    }
}
```

### How is this different from the HashSet approach?

| Feature | Floyd's fast/slow | HashSet approach |
|---|---|---|
| Space | O(1) constant | O(n) for storing all seen nodes |
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Extra data | two pointers | HashSet of node references |
| Finds cycle start | yes (with phase 2) | yes (first repeated node) |
| Preferred when | constant space required | need to identify cycle nodes |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class CycleHashSet {
    public boolean hasCycle(LinkedListCycle.ListNode head) {
        Set<LinkedListCycle.ListNode> seen = new HashSet<>();
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            if (!seen.add(current)) return true; // already seen
            current = current.next;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode t1 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(1);
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode t2 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(2);
        t1.next = t2; t2.next = t1;
        System.out.println("Has cycle (HashSet): " + new CycleHashSet().hasCycle(t1));
    }
}
```

```text
Has cycle (HashSet): true
```

### What are the rules for the null safety check?

Before advancing the fast pointer two steps, check that both `fast` and `fast.next` are non-null. If either is null, the list has a genuine end and there is no cycle. On an even-length acyclic list, `fast` itself may be null. On an odd-length acyclic list, `fast.next` may be null. Checking both covers all cases.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class NullSafetyCheck {
    // while (fast != null && fast.next != null)
    //   fast != null: catches even-length end (fast lands on null)
    //   fast.next != null: catches odd-length end (fast.next is null)

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Even length: 1 -> 2 -> null
        // fast starts at 1, moves to null (even case)
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n1 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(1);
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n2 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(2);
        n1.next = n2;
        System.out.println("Even length: " + new LinkedListCycle().hasCycle(n1));

        // Odd length: 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> null
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n3 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(3);
        n2.next = n3;
        System.out.println("Odd length: " + new LinkedListCycle().hasCycle(n1));
    }
}
```

```text
Even length: false
Odd length: false
```

### How do you find the start of the cycle?

After detecting a meeting point, reset one pointer to the head and keep the other at the meeting point. Advance both one step at a time. They will meet at the cycle start. This works because the distance from the head to the cycle start equals the distance from the meeting point to the cycle start (within the cycle).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CycleStart {
    public LinkedListCycle.ListNode detectCycleStart(LinkedListCycle.ListNode head) {
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode slow = head, fast = head;

        // Phase 1: detect meeting point
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next.next;
            if (slow == fast) break;
        }
        if (fast == null || fast.next == null) return null; // no cycle

        // Phase 2: find cycle start
        slow = head;
        while (slow != fast) {
            slow = slow.next;
            fast = fast.next;
        }
        return slow; // cycle start
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n1 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(1);
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n2 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(2);
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n3 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(3);
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n4 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(4);
        n1.next = n2; n2.next = n3; n3.next = n4; n4.next = n2;

        LinkedListCycle.ListNode start = new CycleStart().detectCycleStart(n1);
        System.out.println("Cycle starts at node: " + (start != null ? start.val : "none"));
    }
}
```

```text
Cycle starts at node: 2
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include an empty list (null head), a single node with no cycle, a single node pointing to itself, and a cycle involving all nodes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CycleEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedListCycle solver = new LinkedListCycle();

        // Empty list
        System.out.println("Empty: " + solver.hasCycle(null));

        // Single node, no cycle
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode single = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(1);
        System.out.println("Single no cycle: " + solver.hasCycle(single));

        // Single node, self-loop
        single.next = single;
        System.out.println("Self-loop: " + solver.hasCycle(single));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: false
Single no cycle: false
Self-loop: true
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is checking only `fast != null` without also checking `fast.next != null`, which causes a NullPointerException when advancing two steps on an odd-length list. Another mistake is comparing node values instead of node references, which would falsely detect a cycle when two different nodes have the same value.

Wrong code:

```java
// WRONG: missing fast.next null check
while (fast != null) {  // should also check fast.next
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next; // NullPointerException on odd-length list
}
```

Correct code:

```java
// CORRECT: check both fast and fast.next
while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow == fast) return true;
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows that Floyd's algorithm extends to finding the cycle start (phase 2) and computing the cycle length. They also know the mathematical proof: if the tail length is mu and the cycle length is C, at the meeting point the slow pointer has traveled mu + k steps inside the cycle, and resetting one pointer to the head and advancing both by one gives the cycle start in mu steps. In production at National Bank of India, the integrity checker uses this to detect circular references in linked transaction chains on memory-constrained infrastructure.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SeniorCycleNotes {
    // Cycle length: after meeting, keep one pointer fixed, advance the other
    // counting steps until they meet again. Count = cycle length.

    public static int cycleLength(LinkedListCycle.ListNode meetingPoint) {
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode current = meetingPoint.next;
        int length = 1;
        while (current != meetingPoint) {
            current = current.next;
            length++;
        }
        return length;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n1 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(1);
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n2 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(2);
        LinkedListCycle.ListNode n3 = new LinkedListCycle.ListNode(3);
        n1.next = n2; n2.next = n3; n3.next = n2; // cycle of length 2

        // Find meeting point first
        System.out.println("Cycle length: " + cycleLength(n2));
    }
}
```

```text
Cycle length: 2
```
